diagram='''<svg viewBox="0 0 960 420" role="img" aria-label="Two pack positives pass through separate contacts of one external DPST switch, then join VBAT ahead of the branch fuses; both negatives share ground">
<g fill="white" stroke="#26393f" stroke-width="2"><rect x="15" y="50" width="150" height="70"/><rect x="15" y="240" width="150" height="70"/><rect x="350" y="20" width="270" height="320"/><rect x="770" y="60" width="170" height="70"/><rect x="770" y="240" width="170" height="70"/></g>
<g fill="#26393f" font-family="system-ui" font-size="20"><text x="35" y="80">Pack A</text><text x="35" y="106">J1.2 positive</text><text x="35" y="270">Pack B</text><text x="35" y="296">J4.2 positive</text><text x="370" y="52">External DPST switch</text><text x="370" y="70">One handle, two poles</text><text x="206" y="102">J5.1</text><text x="635" y="102">J5.2</text><text x="206" y="292">J5.3</text><text x="635" y="292">J5.4</text><text x="787" y="88">F1 → motors</text><text x="787" y="114">30A branch fuse</text><text x="787" y="268">F2 → converters</text><text x="787" y="294">30A branch fuse</text><text x="735" y="205">VBAT</text><text x="365" y="325" font-size="18">Contacts shown OPEN / OFF</text><text x="18" y="390">J1.1 and J4.1 negatives share board GND in both switch positions.</text></g>
<g fill="none" stroke="#9a382f" stroke-width="5"><path d="M165 115H435L490 80M510 115H720V275H770M720 115V95H770"/><path d="M165 305H435L490 270M510 305H720V275"/></g><g stroke="#26393f" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="8 7"><path d="M478 140V235"/></g></svg>'''
body=f'''<p><strong>Robot PDB v0.6 · Engineering prototype · November 9, 2026</strong></p><h1>Review before purchasing</h1>
<p>The board changes implement the simple mechanical disconnect you chose. <strong>Stop the robot, then turn the switch OFF.</strong> One handle opens both battery-positive paths. There is no software supervisor, hold-up sequence, automatic pack-loss detection or powered braking guarantee.</p>
<nav><a href="#wiring">Switch wiring</a> · <a href="#changes">Changes</a> · <a href="#board">Actual board</a> · <a href="#checks">Checks</a> · <a href="#purchase">Purchase review</a> · <a href="#bench">First power-up</a></nav>
<section id="wiring"><h2>What you actually connect</h2>{diagram}
<p><strong>This is four heavy switch wires, not a two-wire Molex control lead.</strong> Use one electrically separate switch pole between J5.1 and J5.2, and the other between J5.3 and J5.4. The two outputs join VBAT on the PCB. Each J5 terminal has two equivalent plated holes; they represent four electrical terminals, not eight separate circuits.</p>
<table><tr><th>Board connection</th><th>Destination / meaning</th></tr><tr><td>J1.2 / J1.1</td><td>Pack A positive / common ground. Source fuse belongs near the battery, ahead of the exposed lead.</td></tr><tr><td>J4.2 / J4.1</td><td>Pack B positive / common ground. Independently source-fused.</td></tr><tr><td>J5.1 → switch pole A → J5.2</td><td>Pack A positive in → shared positive out.</td></tr><tr><td>J5.3 → switch pole B → J5.4</td><td>Pack B positive in → shared positive out.</td></tr><tr><td>J2.2 / J2.1</td><td>Fused motor positive / ground, to the Flipsky input.</td></tr><tr><td>J301/J302 and J401/J402</td><td>Pin 1 ground, pin 2 power, pin 3 data. XL330 is 5V; XL430 is 10.8V despite the legacy +12V_SRV net name.</td></tr></table>
<p>Use a <strong>DC-rated, double-pole, single-throw disconnect</strong>. The Albright ED252 is a datasheet-backed external candidate; ED250 is single-pole and is not equivalent. Confirm its exact mounting variant, terminals, protective cover and capacitive make duty before obtaining the harness. Mount it separately from this PCB. The ED252 manufacturer specifies emergency interruption/no-load isolation, rather than regular loaded switching.</p>
<p>OFF disconnects both battery positives from the board and from each other. <strong>The battery negatives remain common.</strong> Check both contacts with the packs removed before applying power. Check isolation again with every USB/telemetry lead connected. USB can still power the USB section when battery power is OFF.</p>
<p>ON directly parallels the packs. The PCB does not equalize their charge or limit pack-to-pack current. Start commissioning with one current-limited supply. Two-pack operation needs the actual pack/BMS parallel/reconnect limits and measured equalization/inrush; do not use unequal packs as an experiment.</p></section>
<section id="changes"><h2>What changed after Gemini's review</h2><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Implemented</th><th>What remains to establish</th></tr>'''+''.join('<tr>'+''.join(f'<td>{x}</td>' for x in r)+'</tr>' for r in rows)+f'''</table>
<p>Gemini's major findings were checked against pad nets and manufacturer documents. Rejected instructions include changing output-feedback resistors as though they were UVLO resistors, using SMBJ11A as a below-12V servo clamp, assuming a single ideal-diode MOSFET gives OFF isolation, reducing the electronics fuse to 20A while retaining the full load envelope, and treating guessed fault current or a fixed via count as proof.</p>
<p>No active servo brake clamp is fitted. Use an independent voltage-limited energy sink during braking characterization. A servo's supply connector can also connect an external qualified clamp. If measured energy or voltage exceeds the allowed envelope, add a suitable active sink before motion; a TVS with a clamp rating above the actuator maximum does not solve this.</p></section>
<section id="board"><h2>The actual v0.6 board</h2><p>The board is 228 × 108mm. These images are exported from the checked v0.6 PCB; the older course's interactive board reader is v0.4.1.</p><figure><a href="v06-assets/assembly-top.svg"><img src="v06-assets/assembly-top.svg" alt="Actual v0.6 top assembly drawing with part references"></a><figcaption>Top assembly drawing. Open the image to zoom into component identifiers and polarity.</figcaption></figure><details><summary>Show filled top copper</summary><img src="v06-assets/board-top.svg" alt="Filled v0.6 top copper and terminal labels"></details><details><summary>Show filled bottom copper (viewed through the board)</summary><img src="v06-assets/board-bottom.svg" alt="Filled v0.6 bottom copper"></details></section>
<section id="checks"><h2>Automated checks and their limits</h2><table><tr><th>Check</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Native KiCad 7.0.11 refill and DRC</td><td>{check}. The three warnings are C3/C4/J3 silkscreen artwork moved to the fabrication layer; pad and drill geometry is unchanged.</td></tr><tr><td>Fresh schematic-to-board comparison</td><td>{v['compared_multi_terminal_nets']} multi-terminal nets agree. Every numbered PCB pad and footprint identity also matches the circuit inventory.</td></tr><tr><td>OFF-isolation topology</td><td>Each pack-positive net contains only its battery input and its own J5 input. Only the switch-output terminals join VBAT. Physical wiring/continuity still needs inspection.</td></tr><tr><td>Assembly inventory</td><td>{v['jlc_fitted_parts']} proposed JLC-fitted parts in {v['bom_lines']} BOM lines. F1/F2, and power wiring are separate hand-fit items.</td></tr><tr><td>Factory outputs</td><td>Fresh four-layer Gerbers, paste, masks, silkscreen, outline, plated and non-plated drills, BOM and CPL generated from the checked source. Source hashes are recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical-rule checking</td><td>KiCad 7 has no automated ERC command here, and the generated custom symbols use passive pins. This is not a comprehensive ERC pass. Independent net, pad, footprint and critical topology checks provide different evidence.</td></tr></table>
<p>A clean CAD check cannot show whether the power stage is stable, a fuse clears quickly enough, the enclosure overheats, a servo rail survives regeneration, or USB passes its electrical tests. Those require measurements.</p></section>
<section id="purchase"><h2>Before you pull the trigger</h2><p><strong>Purchase scope: limited engineering prototype.</strong> This is not a qualified robot power assembly. The website draft and actual placement preview must match these files before payment. Review quantities and total cost on the site; no payment has been made.</p>
<ul><li>Fabricate 5 boards / assemble the smallest practical subset (target 2) unless the live quote offers a better minimum. All proposed factory assembly is on the top side.</li><li>Four layers, nominal 1.6mm, 2oz outer / 1oz inner, ENIG, <strong>JLC041621-2116</strong>. Manufacturer construction is about 1.648mm, ±10%. L2 is the reference ground plane.</li><li>Require production-file confirmation and impedance review. The Gerber bundle includes the USB requirements image. Check the factory's final construction and adjusted traces before approving production.</li><li>Review polarized placements: power IC pin 1, Q1/Q2/Q3, diodes, electrolytics, USB connector and all headers. The uploaded CPL begins with package-convention offsets; the actual factory preview decides whether they are correct.</li><li>Order the two exact axial fuses separately and hand solder after SMT. The Thor inductor is the stocked Coilcraft XAL1510-103MED, with its matching footprint; check its 10mm height. Obtain the external dual disconnect, source-end protection, covered terminals, strain relief, soldered power pigtails and custom servo harnesses separately. They are not silently included in the PCBA price.</li><li>Use the hole pattern and actual tall parts to check standoff height, bottom clearance, cable bends and external-switch fit before enclosing it.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Would I order it?</strong> I would order the small engineering batch after the factory placement and impedance review are acceptable, for current-limited bench qualification. I would not install it on the loaded robot or connect two unrestricted battery packs on the strength of a clean DRC.</p></section>
<section id="bench"><h2>Commission in this order</h2><ol><li><strong>Unpowered inspection.</strong> Check polarity, solder bridges, connector mating, fuse identity, switch contact mapping and isolation. Verify resistance/diode behavior at each rail with USB absent and attached. Provide external strain relief before using heavy leads.</li><li><strong>Current-limited startup with dummy loads.</strong> Begin with one protected supply and a low current limit, then increase deliberately. Check nominal 24.33V Thor, approximately 5V branches, 10.8V servo and approximately 11.7V audio. Verify every rail before connecting valuable equipment. Record startup, ripple, load steps, current limit and dropout.</li><li><strong>Protection and thermal envelope.</strong> Measure drop and temperature at both positive/negative inputs, all four switch paths, fuse leads, Q1 transitions, Thor output and servo/audio feeds. Test enclosure conditions. The 30A fuse's standard 75% continuous screen is 22.5A before temperature correction; its high interruption rating does not prove rapid moderate-overload clearing or downstream wire protection. Use controlled protection testing with suitable equipment, not an unbounded battery short.</li><li><strong>Reduced-power drive only after power checks.</strong> Initial budget is 4A battery current per ESC channel and 10A total electronics input, approximately 18A combined. Read back the installed controller settings. These are required operating limits, not limits automatically enforced by this PCB. Do not assume equal pack sharing.</li><li><strong>Pack reconnection and braking.</strong> Establish source-fuse/harness coordination and the actual pack/BMS parallel limits before two-pack use. Bound switch make current, contact bounce and recharge current. Characterize full-pack, one-pack and total-disconnect cases with an independent energy sink and bounded test energy. Zero programmed battery regen alone is not proof of a safe stop. Accept coast after total disconnect only after measuring stopping distance.</li><li><strong>Servo and USB tests.</strong> Restrict XL330 total current below the shared 5A supply limit; four simultaneous stalls total about 5.88A. Test cable-end voltage, jam limits, rail regeneration below actuator maxima and TTL turnaround. Measure USB attach current, preconfiguration/suspend consumption, both plug orientations, resume and concurrent audio/servo traffic.</li></ol>
<p>The full simultaneous screening envelope is approximately 920W battery input at assumed 85% conversion/drive efficiency: about 32A at 28.8V and 38.3A at 24V. A single pack's published 30A continuous rating does not cover that envelope at nominal/low voltage. The prototype's restricted 18A bring-up mode is a different operating case. Higher power requires measured limits and hardware/firmware qualification.</p></section>
<section id="wheel-option"><h2>Optional DDSM350-CT wheels</h2><p>These wheels are an option study, not a change to this order. Each has its own drive electronics and encoder. One USB-to-CAN adapter can control two addressed motors; no additional Flipsky or H-bridge is needed. CAN avoids connecting the uncertain TTL232 logic thresholds directly to Jetson pins.</p><p>Waveshare publishes 24V nominal but no allowed input range or absolute maximum on the inspected pages. The raw motor output reaches 33.6V, so direct connection needs manufacturer confirmation. Do not share the Thor converter's 8A output without redesigning the budget. A separate regulated motor supply must also handle returned braking energy; a generic buck is not automatically an energy sink.</p><p>The live quantity-two price on November 9 is $108.39 each, $216.78 for the pair. Allow roughly $35–70 for CAN/protected wiring if raw 8S compatibility is confirmed, or $150–300 for regulated 24V power, braking provision, CAN and harness integration. Thus budget about $250–290 or $370–520 including the wheels, before shipping, tax, mounts and labor. These are engineering allowances, not qualified supply quotes.</p><p>Plan a few focused bench/integration days, plus loaded braking and thermal tests. If replacing the present wheels, note that 1.1Nm at 270rpm calculates to about 31W rated mechanical output per wheel, compared with the present 200W nameplates. Sustained 4mph under load is unproven. If adding them, the pair adds about 2.57kg plus supply and mounting hardware.</p><p>For a first trial, use an external protected add-on fed from J2, with its own qualified power conversion and CAN wiring. Integrating the motor converter on this PCB would require another significant power-stage layout revision. <a href="https://www.waveshare.com/ddsm350-ct.htm">Official wheel listing</a> · <a href="https://docs.waveshare.com/DDSM350-CT">Specifications</a> · <a href="https://docs.waveshare.com/DDSM350-CT/CAN-Use">CAN protocol</a>.</p></section><section><h2>Evidence and further reading</h2><p><a href="ROBOT_PDB_LEARNING_REPORT.html">Illustrated circuit course (historical board, current introduction)</a> · <a href="../outputs/v06-order-preparation/validation.json">Exact automated results and file hashes</a> · <a href="../revisions/v0.6/drc.rpt">Native DRC report</a> · <a href="../outputs/v06-order-preparation/hand-fit-and-harness.csv">Hand-fit shopping list</a> · <a href="../outputs/gemini-v05-review/verified-findings.txt">Full verified Gemini reconciliation</a></p><p>Manufacturer references: <a href="https://www.albrightinternational.com/wpcms/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ED252-Data-Sheet.pdf">Albright ED252</a>; <a href="https://www.littelfuse.com/~/media/electronics/datasheets/fuses/littelfuse_fuse_505_datasheet.pdf.pdf">Littelfuse 505</a>; <a href="https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps22918.pdf">TI TPS22918</a>; <a href="https://www.coilcraft.com/getmedia/cd1cef27-13f0-4568-8894-f7311475209b/xal1510.pdf">Coilcraft L1</a>; <a href="https://www.sunlordinc.com/uploads/files/20221116/UPZ%20series%20of%20Multilayer%20Chip%20Ferrite%20Bead.pdf">Sunlord beads</a>; <a href="https://jlcpcb.com/impedance">JLC stackups</a>; <a href="https://jlcpcb.com/pcb-impedance-calculator">JLC impedance calculator</a>.</p></section>'''
body = '<aside style="padding:14px;border:2px solid #926326;background:#fffaf0"><strong>Archived v0.6 wheel design.</strong> This page describes the previous board. <a href="PROTOTYPE_v0.7_WALKTHROUGH.html">Open the current replacement-wheel / USB3 walkthrough.</a> The earlier factory draft is superseded.</aside>' + re.sub(r'<section id="wheel-option">.*?</section>', '<section id="wheel-option"><h2>Replacement-wheel revision</h2><p>The DDSM350-CT option was selected and is now being integrated on main, with internal CAN, USB3 and PWM control. <a href="PROTOTYPE_v0.7_WALKTHROUGH.html">See the current design and release status.</a> Previous cost allowances are not a quote for that design.</p></section>', body, flags=re.S)
